  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1250 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nSaturday night through Thursday:\n\nWeak low pressure continues east from the Great Lakes region on\nSunday, likely deepening slightly and dragging a cold front across\nWI. The exact arrival time for the front is not yet certain, but\nthe initial NW wind shift is currently favored for the early morning\n(predawn) hours, followed by the arrival of lower dewpoints \n(low 60s or potentially even 50s) on these same winds and / or a\nsecondary backdoor cold front (with NE winds) Sunday afternoon.\nIn the 500mb pattern, we do have a 500mb shortwave trough that \ncould phase well with the warm sector over IN / IL Sunday \nafternoon, but that doesn't leave us much to work with in \nsouthern WI. A round of showers or weak thunderstorms along the \ninitial frontal passage Saturday night into Sunday morning would\nalso (potentially) leave some cloud cover to interfere with \npeak heating, limiting the instability when the afternoon \nsecondary baroclinic zone / backdoor cold front arrives. All \nthis to say Sunday's storm setup leaves a lot in question, but \nthe odds of any strong to severe storms are relatively low for \nnow. Overall rain chances 50-65%, peaking early Sunday morning\nand tapering into the afternoon. \n\nPredominantly dry weather expected Sunday night into Tuesday\nmorning as high pressure passes overhead, with temperatures near\nseasonal norms. Shower and thunderstorm chances return late\nTuesday through midweek as a surface trough of low pressure sweeps\neast across the midwest. \n\nSheppard",
